toolCallRounds.response:
The page reloads because your login button is inside a `<form>` element. Even though you call `event.preventDefault()` on the button's click event, the **form still submits** and reloads the page.

Two ways to fix this:

### Option 1: Listen on the form's `submit` event instead (recommended)

````javascript
// filepath: c:\Users\nlondhe\class_coding\s26-michelin\frontend\js\login.js
// ...existing code...

function initLoginPage() {
    const form = document.getElementById("login-form"); // or whatever your <form> id is
    form.addEventListener("submit", handleLoginSubmit);
}

// ...existing code...
````

### Option 2: Add `type="button"` to your button in HTML

If your button is `<button>Login</button>` inside a form, the browser treats it as a submit button by default. Change it to:

```html
<button type="button" id="login-button">Login</button>
```

This prevents the form from submitting when the button is clicked, so your existing `click` listener will work and you'll see the console logs.

**Option 1 is the better approach** — it also handles the user pressing Enter in the form fields.

